<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ante Trumbić</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1864">1864</a>–<a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ante Trumbić was a Croatian politician from Split who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a>, the exile body of South Slav politicians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. In 1917 he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corfu-declaration">Corfu Declaration</a> with the Serbian prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-pasic">Nikola Pašić</a>, agreeing on a united kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and he became the new state's first foreign minister. He later opposed Serbian centralism.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ante-trumbic.jpg" alt="Ante Trumbić" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arthur W. Page · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Ante_Trumbi%C4%87.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
